# LibreWolf update-check.patch  (Android)
# Owner: LW-M6-06 ("Implement in-app update checking without an app store")
#
# Upstream files touched
# -----------------------
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/build.gradle                                   (2 buildConfigFields)
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/HomeActivity.kt (1 call in onResume)
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/settings/SettingsFragment.kt (row visibility + binding)
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/res/xml/preferences.xml                (the switch row, not persisted)
# New files
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/lw/UpdateCheck.kt
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/res/values/lw_update_check.xml
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/test/java/org/mozilla/fenix/lw/UpdateCheckerTest.kt
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/test/java/org/mozilla/fenix/lw/UpdateCheckSwitchTest.kt
#
# Line numbers are from the ESR tree (firefox-153.0esr, ./version.android), AFTER
# the patches listed before this one in assets/patches/android.txt.
#
#
# What it is
# -----------
# The direct-APK distribution has no store to tell its users an update exists.
# docs/android/DISTRIBUTION.md is the contract; this is the code. In one
# paragraph: a switch in Settings (About category), OFF by default, with a
# summary that says exactly what is sent and where. When it is on, and only
# then, HomeActivity.onResume asks UpdateCheck.maybeRun, which at most once per
# 24 h fetches two static files from the distribution host -- latest.json and
# latest.json.sig -- verifies the ECDSA P-256 / SHA-256 signature against a
# public key embedded at build time, and if the signed document names a newer
# version than the running one, shows a dialog with an "Open download page"
# button. It never downloads, never installs, never retries in a loop, and
# treats every failure (network, parse, signature) as "no update".
#
#
# The privacy properties, and where each one is pinned
# -----------------------------------------------------
#   * Nothing identifies the install. The request carries a static User-Agent
#     ("Redoubt-UpdateCheck/1", the same literal on every install), no cookies
#     (CookiePolicy.OMIT), no cache validators (useCaches=false, so no ETag or
#     If-Modified-Since that could act as a per-install token), follows no
#     redirects, and is a private load. It removes the Accept-Language header
#     Gecko would add (the user's locale -- 157.0-2 sent it, "en-US" in its
#     device acceptance) and Accept, by setting them empty, which necko treats
#     as "remove" (UpdateChecker.REQUEST_HEADERS says why, and which headers
#     Gecko adds later that a caller cannot remove: Sec-GPC, Sec-Fetch-*,
#     Priority, ...; all constant). It sends NOT EVEN the version string:
#     the comparison happens on the device, so the server learns only that
#     some Redoubt asked -- from the connection's IP address, at that time.
#     The server is GitHub Pages (redoubtbrowser.org is hosted there), so that
#     is what GitHub sees, once a day while the switch is on. UpdateCheckerTest pins every one of those request
#     fields, because a packet capture cannot see inside TLS.
#   * Off by default, opt-in, disclosed. android:defaultValue="false"; the
#     summary names the host and who runs it (GitHub Pages), the cadence,
#     what is and is not sent.
#   * Foreground only, rate-limited. The only caller is onResume; there is no
#     WorkManager job, timer or service. UpdateCheck.MIN_INTERVAL_MS = 24 h,
#     tracked in its own SharedPreferences file (lw_update_check).
#   * Store builds do not double-notify, by ARTIFACT not by runtime query.
#     build.gradle reads -PlwUpdateCheckPubkey; without it
#     BuildConfig.LW_UPDATE_CHECK_PUBKEY is "", UpdateCheck.isCompiledIn is
#     false, SettingsFragment hides the row, and maybeRun returns on its first
#     line. The F-Droid and Accrescent pipelines simply do not pass the key.
#     scripts/android-apk.sh forwards LW_UPDATE_CHECK_PUBKEY (and, for the
#     smoke harness, LW_UPDATE_CHECK_ENDPOINT) from the environment.
#   * ...and store INSTALLS of the direct APK do not either (2026-10-05). The
#     own F-Droid repository (LW-M6-03) serves the GitHub APKs unchanged, check
#     compiled in. UpdateCheck.isOffered asks the installer of record
#     (InstallSourceInfo.installingPackageName, API 30+; getInstallerPackageName
#     before): F-Droid / F-Droid Basic / Droid-ify / Neo Store / Accrescent /
#     Play -> row hidden, isEnabled false whatever is stored. Nothing is sent
#     anywhere; the installer is read only when the switch is on.
#   * The private key never enters the build or this repository. What the
#     build embeds is the base64 DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo. Generating and
#     holding the key is LW-M6-01's custody problem, not this patch's.
#
#
# Why a detached .sig and not a "signature" field inside the JSON
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# DISTRIBUTION.md's first draft put the signature inside the document, "over
# this document". A signature over a JSON object needs a canonical form (which
# whitespace, which key order, how numbers are written) agreed between the
# maintainer's signing script and the Kotlin verifier, and a mismatch there is
# a silent "no update" forever. Signing the exact served bytes and shipping the
# signature next to them (the minisign/signify shape) has no canonical form to
# get wrong: `openssl dgst -sha256 -sign key.pem latest.json | base64 -w0 >
# latest.json.sig`. DISTRIBUTION.md is updated to match.
#
#
# Why ECDSA P-256 and not Ed25519
# --------------------------------
# java.security.Signature "Ed25519" exists only from API 33; SHA256withECDSA
# and the EC KeyFactory are in every Android release Fenix supports, and
# openssl produces and verifies the same signatures on the maintainer's side.
#
#
# What this patch does NOT do, on purpose
# ----------------------------------------
#   * It does not create the endpoint. The document is generated by
#     scripts/update-manifest.py, signed by the maintainer with
#     scripts/sign-update-manifest.sh and published under site/update/android/
#     (https://redoubtbrowser.org/update/android/latest.json). A build made
#     without a key has no reachable code; a build made with a key while
#     nothing is published there gets NoResult and stays silent.
#   * No key is checked in. The release pipeline passes the PUBLIC key when the
#     maintainer has one.
#   * No notification-channel plumbing: the prompt is an in-app dialog on the
#     home activity, which needs no permission on any API level.
#
#
# Ordering
# ---------
# Shares fenix app/build.gradle with no-adjust, no-glean, no-gms,
# no-crashreporter, fenix-abi-split and branding (an ORDERED chain);
# HomeActivity.kt with no-nimbus, no-adjust and no-suggest. preferences.xml,
# SettingsFragment.kt and the three new files are shared with nothing. Every
# pair was replayed from the pristine ESR copy in list order and with this
# patch moved in front of each partner; results byte-identical, exit 0, no .rej
# -- see scripts/check-patch-order.py's REVIEWED_ORDER_FREE. Listed last in
# assets/patches/android.txt.
#
#
# Verification
# -------------
#   ./mach gradle fenix:testDebugUnitTest  -> UpdateCheckerTest (13 tests)
#     and UpdateCheckSwitchTest (7 tests: the switch and the check agree;
#     a store installer hides the row and keeps the check off)
#   scripts/tests/test-update-check-jvm.sh  -> the same 11 tests plus a
#     cross-check against openssl-signed scripts/update-manifest.py output,
#     on the host JVM with -Werror and stubbed Fenix classes (no tree build);
#     it cannot run UpdateCheckSwitchTest (no Android runtime), it only greps
#     that the switch wiring is still in this patch
#   ./scripts/android-smoke.sh --check-update-privacy --serial <dev> --apk <apk>
#     with the switch OFF: no traffic to the update host across launch and the
#     settings screens; with it ON (flipped through the UI): exactly one host
#     is contacted, the update host, and nothing else new. Store-style builds
#     (no key) pass because the row does not exist and nothing is sent.
#   Evidence: docs/android/evidence/lw-m6-06/.
#
# 157 rebase (2026-10-02, Firefox release 157.0; the ESR line-number note above
# is historical)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * SettingsFragment.kt: setupCookieBannerPreference() is gone upstream (the
#     cookie banner service was removed in 156), so the row-visibility block now
#     sits after the secret-debug-menu `with(settings)` block, in front of
#     setupInstallAddonFromFilePreference(). Same statement, same effect.
#   * UpdateCheck.kt: the running version is read through upstream's
#     packageManagerCompatHelper.getPackageInfoCompat() instead of
#     PackageManager.getPackageInfo(String, Int), which is @Deprecated from
#     API 33 and therefore a -Werror hazard.
#   * UpdateCheck.isNewer: on the release track the Firefox version and the
#     Redoubt release must be compared separately ("157.0.1-1" > "157.0-2"; a
#     flat digit list said the opposite). Firefox components first, then the
#     part after the last '-'. ESR-era strings still order correctly, and a
#     153.x esr install is offered 157.
#   * UpdateCheckerTest: fixtures moved to 157.0-N; release-track and
#     ESR-to-release ordering cases added (still 8 tests).
#   * build.gradle, HomeActivity.kt, preferences.xml: context only (offsets).
#
# Publishing side (2026-10-04, before the first build with a key)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------
#   * The versionCode decides. Every Redoubt APK's versionName is
#     "<firefox>-<release>-default" and betas share it (beta.4 and beta.5 are
#     both "157.0-1-default"), so a check on versionName alone could never
#     announce the next beta, and isNewer reads the "-1" of "157.0-1-default"
#     as a Firefox component ("157.0-2-default" vs "157.0.1-1-default" orders
#     backwards). The document now carries `version_code`, the LOWEST
#     versionCode among the release's APKs (Fenix's build-hour code, which
#     Android requires to increase on every update); when both it and the
#     install's own code (PackageInfoCompat.getLongVersionCode, as upstream's
#     AboutFragment reads it) are known, an update is offered iff the
#     document's code is greater. A document with a code and an install whose
#     own code reads 0 is "up to date" (the strings would offer a beta its own
#     release); only a document without a code falls back to the strings.
#     Nothing new is sent: the comparison is still on the device.
#   * Default endpoint moved from /updates/android/ to /update/android/, the
#     path the website publishes (docs/android/WEBSITE.md, DISTRIBUTION.md).
#   * UpdateCheckerTest: three versionCode cases (11 tests).
#   * Disclosure: the switch summary says who answers the request -- GitHub
#     Pages serves redoubtbrowser.org, so GitHub sees the requesting IP address
#     and the time, once a day while the switch is on.
#
# The switch never reached the check (2026-10-05, found in 157.0-2 acceptance)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * Defect. The row was a plain SwitchPreferenceCompat. SettingsFragment sets
#     no sharedPreferencesName, so androidx persisted it to the DEFAULT file
#     (org.redoubtbrowser_preferences.xml), while UpdateCheck.isEnabled read
#     components.settings.preferences (fenix_preferences). The switch turned on
#     and showed ON; the check never ran. Device acceptance of 157.0-2 caught it
#     (docs/android/evidence/lw-m7-01/release-157.0-2/acceptance/update-check/).
#   * Fix, the way upstream wires its switches that live in fenix_preferences
#     (CustomizationFragment's gesture switches): UpdateCheck.bindSwitch, called
#     from SettingsFragment.setupPreferences, sets isChecked from isEnabled and
#     puts SharedPreferenceUpdater (which writes components.settings.preferences
#     under the row's key) on the row. The row is android:persistent="false", so
#     androidx keeps no second copy. Binding writes nothing; absent reads as off.
#     A value 157.0-2 left in the default file is ignored, not migrated: only a
#     tap on the row can turn the check on. isEnabled gained a compiledIn
#     parameter (default isCompiledIn) so the test build, which has no key, can
#     ask it.
#   * Why every gate passed. UpdateCheckerTest drives UpdateChecker with every
#     input injected and never touched the switch; test-update-check-jvm.sh runs
#     the same tests on a plain JVM; the patch gates do not compile. On a device,
#     the 2026-09-06 opt-in run (evidence/lw-m7-06) saw no request after the
#     switch was turned on and put that down to nothing being published -- it
#     was this defect. UpdateCheckSwitchTest now drives the row's change listener
#     the way a tap does and asks isEnabled: on after a tap, off after a second
#     tap, off by default, and off with a value only in the default file.
#
# Store installs of the same APK (2026-10-05, LW-M6-03)
# -------------------------------------------------------
#   * Redoubt's own F-Droid repository serves the GitHub release's APKs, byte
#     for byte, so they carry the check (opt-in, off). Without this, an F-Droid
#     user who turned the switch on would be told about each update twice.
#     A second, check-less Gecko build for the repository was the alternative;
#     the installer of record makes it unnecessary.
#   * UpdateCheck.installerOf reads InstallSourceInfo.installingPackageName (the
#     package that made the LAST install or update; API 30+) or, below 30,
#     getInstallerPackageName (@Suppress("DEPRECATION"): -Werror). Measured on
#     an Android 14 emulator with F-Droid 2.0.1: a hand-installed Beta 5 that
#     F-Droid updated to 157.0-2 reads org.fdroid.fdroid; a fresh F-Droid
#     install reads org.fdroid.fdroid with updateOwner org.fdroid.fdroid; an
#     APK installed by hand over it reads the package installer again
#     (docs/android/evidence/lw-m6-03/logs/11-13).
#   * isOffered = compiled in && installer not in STORE_INSTALLERS. bindSwitch
#     hides the row when not offered; isEnabled is false then, whatever the
#     stored value (it is kept, not cleared: a later hand install brings the
#     user's choice back). Unknown installer (adb, uninstalled store) -> offered.
#   * Tests: UpdateCheckerTest pins the list (isStoreInstaller); three new
#     UpdateCheckSwitchTest cases set the installer through Robolectric's
#     ShadowPackageManager.setInstallSourceInfo.

